# Variables whose value identifies this particular lab host.
#
# NAMES ONLY. Never a value. This file is tracked and world-readable.
#
# This is the authority the hygiene gate trusts, and it is a separate file on
# purpose. The gate also wants a `# host-specific` tag next to each variable in
# local.env.example, so a reader sees the rule where the variable is, but a tag
# cannot be the authority: the mistake the gate exists to catch is someone
# typing a real value over the commented declaration, and that single edit
# deletes the tag as a side effect. A gate that infers its own rules from the
# line being edited stops objecting exactly when it matters.
#
# So the two must agree, and the gate reports any disagreement:
#
#   * a name here that is untagged in the example      -> finding
#   * a name tagged in the example but missing here    -> finding
#   * a name here that is assigned a value in the example -> finding
#   * an empty list                                    -> finding
#
# Removing protection therefore takes an edit to this file, which does nothing
# else and reviews in one line.

LAB_HOST_SSH
LAB_WSL_USER
LAB_LAN_CIDR
LAB_LAN_PROBE_IP
